# MoE host CPU compute tier: decompose the 283 us readback, usm_host destinations, one round trip

# Applied against the pinned runtime, build 2026.4.0-22849-71640275d29, on top of
# patches/0001-0016. Implements an unpublished design note's ("design-m14-readback",
# not committed under docs/ -- a working note handed to the implementer, not part of
# this repository's documentation tree) M14 follow-on scope ONLY: the instrumentation
# that decomposes patches/0012's avg_cpu_x_us into an enqueue span, a wait span, an
# env-gated queue-drain probe, and a topk_id-side counter; usm_host destinations for
# the x (hidden_states) and routing-weight readback in place of pageable std::vector
# buffers (§A1); and the hoist of that readback into the existing topk_id round trip,
# so a tier-on layer pays one combined wait instead of two separate ones (§A2). The
# design's phase B (host-visible residency for the MoE node's hidden_states input) is
# deliberately NOT here -- that is 0018, and this patch does not touch HIDDEN_STATES'
# allocation type, the fused-graph residency decision, or anything under
# get_lockable_preferred_memory_allocation_type(). No numeric behaviour change on any
# path: tier OFF is byte-for-byte the code patches/0012 landed (see "Tier-off
# equivalence" below); tier ON computes the exact same host-visible bytes through the
# exact same moe_cpu_expert kernel, only sourced from a usm_host buffer instead of a
# std::vector one and read back in a different order relative to
# try_acquire_simultaneous.
#
# Review round (P17-HOLD, addressed in full before this version):
#   1. tx0 was stamped before the MOE_OTD_READBACK_PROBE drain block and outside
#      `if (_cpu_tier)`. An armed probe run therefore folded its own stream.finish()
#      cost into cpu_topk_id_ns/cpu_x_enq_ns/cpu_x_readback_ns -- exactly the
#      contamination the probe exists to keep separate from the steady measurement.
#      Fixed: tx0 (declared at outer scope, default-constructed) is now stamped
#      inside `if (_cpu_tier)`, after the probe block.
#   2. Added MOE_OTD_READBACK_NOHOIST=1, restoring patches/0012's order (topk_id
#      blocking read, then try_acquire_simultaneous, then a miss-gated x/rw wait) so
#      the hoist's win and the topk_id round trip in isolation are both measurable
#      from one build, by flag, instead of by comparing across patch revisions. With
#      it set, cpu_topk_id_ns measures the blocking topk_id read alone, as the design
#      asked; cpu_x_enq_ns/cpu_x_wait_ns/the retained cpu_x_readback_ns measure the
#      separate, still miss-gated, x/rw wait. moe_otd_perf.hpp's single combined
#      timing function was split into record_cpu_topk_id_timing() and a narrower
#      record_cpu_x_timing() so each leg is recorded independently in both modes,
#      without either function needing to know which mode it is being called from.
#   3. Test changes: the telescoping-identity test's header no longer calls itself
#      red-first -- it is a construction lock (given this patch's actual code there is
#      no reachable call site that violates the invariant; the test locks that fact
#      against a future edit, it does not report a found defect) and now says so. A
#      new host-only test, red against patches/0012's own dump() divisor, is added
#      (see "Unit tests" below). The measurement recipe's step 2 gained two
#      assertions: cpu_tier_calls == 40 (layers) x decode tokens at P*, and
#      cpu_tier_calls > cpu_tier_layers (not every tier-on call has a miss).
#   4. dispatch_cpu_tier() now asserts _cpu_x_host_mem && _cpu_rw_host_mem on entry
#      (OPENVINO_ASSERT, loud rather than a silent null-pointer read if the miss-gating
#      above it ever drifts out of sync with x/rw population). ensure_cpu_host_scratch()
#      gained one sentence on why no pending-wait is needed on regrowth (the previous
#      call's DMAs and CPU workers were already joined by the time this runs again for
#      the next call) and that bytes beyond the current call's request are left
#      uninitialised by design (nothing ever reads past them).
#   5. Header fixes: build-location names removed (this file names no tree or staging
#      directory -- those live in the task's own tracking, not in a patch that ships
#      publicly); the design citation qualified as an unpublished note, not a docs/
#      file; expert_weight_provider.hpp added to "Deviations" below (the
#      uploads_enqueued contract); MOE_OTD_READBACK_PROBE and MOE_OTD_READBACK_NOHOIST
#      both noted as arming on the env var's presence, not on a particular value.
#
# Reference cell P* (design note, reproduced here since every number below is
# meaningless without it): 35B int4, 16 GiB card, otd_ratio 50, 8 GiB device
# pool, 128 slots, u8 KV, one lane, n_ctx 65,536, an 897-token prompt, 64
# greedy decode tokens. Recorded pre-0017: tier ON 15.0-15.5 t/s vs tier OFF
# 10.4-10.6 t/s; per tier-layer decomposition dispatch-to-join 307/396 us +
# x/routing readback 283/286 us + y writeback 29/40 us + pool wake 5/8 us,
# 3.21 host experts per layer.
#
# What changed and why (moe/moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp,
# on_before_batched_gemv):
#
#   Before (patches/0001-0012). Per MoE layer, in program order: (1) a
#   BLOCKING copy_to() reads scratch.topk_id back to host, uncounted by any
#   perf counter; (2) try_acquire_simultaneous() resolves each expert to a
#   device slot or a CPU-tier sentinel, redirecting capacity misses to
#   _cpu_tier_misses; (3) a BLOCKING copy_from() uploads the remapped slot
#   ids; (4) IF there were any misses, hidden_states and the routing weights
#   are read back into two std::vector<uint16_t> buffers via non-blocking
#   copy_to() calls joined by one wait_for_events -- this span is
#   cpu_x_readback_ns, averaging 283 us at P*, an amount patches/0012's own
#   header called "anomalous...not explicable by transfer" for 4 KiB.
#
#   After (patches/0017, default -- MOE_OTD_READBACK_NOHOIST unset). Step (1)'s
#   topk_id read and step (4)'s x/routing-weight read are merged: when the CPU
#   tier is configured on, all three copy_to() calls (topk_id, hidden_states,
#   routing weights) are enqueued non-blocking together and joined with ONE
#   wait_for_events, run SPECULATIVELY -- before this layer is known to have
#   any capacity miss at all, trading a ~4 KiB DMA on every tier-on layer
#   (well under 1 us of PCIe transfer at this size per the design's own
#   arithmetic) for skipping what used to be a second, separate round trip on
#   every layer that did miss. Steps (2) and (3) are otherwise unchanged. x
#   and the routing weights now land in usm_host (GTT) engine memory
#   (allocation_type::usm_host, the same pattern moe_otd_runtime.cpp's
#   StagingRing already uses for its DMA staging slots) instead of
#   std::vector, removing the pageable-destination pin/bounce hypothesis
#   (design's H1) as a candidate for the cost. topk_id's own destination
#   stays a plain std::vector<uint32_t>: A1 is scoped to the x/routing-weight
#   bundle only (the design's own wording), and try_acquire_simultaneous's
#   interface takes a std::vector<uint32_t> either way.
#
#   MOE_OTD_READBACK_NOHOIST=1 restores steps (1) and (4) to their pre-0017
#   order and gating (topk_id alone and blocking; x/rw miss-gated, after the
#   slot-index upload, exactly where 0012 had them) while keeping A1's
#   usm_host destinations -- the flag reorders the reads, it does not revert
#   their memory type. This lets the same build isolate the topk_id round
#   trip's own cost (cpu_topk_id_ns under this flag) and separately confirm
#   the hoist's saving (compare cpu_x_readback_ns with the flag set vs unset).
#
#   Tier OFF: none of the above runs. topk_id's copy_to() is the exact
#   pre-existing blocking call, unedited, in an `else` branch parallel to the
#   tier-on block -- not a flag-gated variant of new code, the literal
#   patches/0012 line. This is the equivalence baseline's structural
#   guarantee, not merely an intended one: a diff of this function's tier-off
#   path against patches/0012 is empty.
#
#   Consequence, stated rather than hidden (moe_otd_perf.hpp's comments on
#   record_cpu_topk_id_timing/record_cpu_x_timing say the same thing):
#   hoisted, cpu_topk_id_ns and the retained cpu_x_readback_ns measure the
#   same merged span -- but by the CALLER's choice to pass both functions the
#   same (tx0, t_wait_done) pair, not by anything internal to either
#   function; under MOE_OTD_READBACK_NOHOIST the caller passes them genuinely
#   different spans and the two counters diverge, which is the whole point of
#   the flag.
#
# New counters (OtdPerfCounters, moe_otd_perf.hpp; all read 0 with the tier
# off, and read 0 with the tier on until MOE_OTD_PERF_LOG=1 is set the same
# way every existing OTD counter does):
#   cpu_tier_calls      - every tier-on on_before_batched_gemv call (not just
#                          the ones with a miss: the readback is speculative
#                          by default). Divides avg_cpu_topk_id_us/
#                          avg_cpu_x_enq_us/avg_cpu_x_wait_us/avg_cpu_x_us
#                          together with cpu_x_warm_layers (see below) -- NOT
#                          cpu_tier_layers (patches/0011's miss-only counter,
#                          unchanged and still the divisor for
#                          avg_cpu_compute_us/avg_cpu_join_wait_us/
#                          avg_cpu_y_us, none of which this patch touches).
#   cpu_topk_id_ns       - the topk_id leg's span (default: merged with x/rw,
#                          tx0 to the combined wait's return; under
#                          MOE_OTD_READBACK_NOHOIST: tx0 to the blocking
#                          topk_id read's own return), steady-state calls
#                          only. us: avg_cpu_topk_id_us.
#   cpu_x_enq_ns         - tx0 (of the x/rw leg) to the last relevant
#                          copy_to() call returning: driver submission time,
#                          steady-state calls only. us: avg_cpu_x_enq_us.
#   cpu_x_wait_ns        - the x/rw leg's wait_for_events call alone, steady-
#                          state calls only. us: avg_cpu_x_wait_us.
#                          cpu_x_enq_ns + cpu_x_wait_ns == the retained
#                          cpu_x_readback_ns, enforced by construction
#                          (record_cpu_x_timing computes all three from the
#                          same three timestamps) and checked by this patch's
#                          unit test.
#   cpu_x_drain_ns       - env-gated (MOE_OTD_READBACK_PROBE=1 -- arms on the
#                          variable's presence, any value, not a particular
#                          one, same convention as MOE_OTD_FORCE_FINISH/
#                          MOE_OTD_READBACK_NOHOIST): a stream.finish() timed
#                          immediately before tx0. us: avg_cpu_x_drain_us,
#                          divided by cpu_tier_calls (the probe runs on every
#                          tier-on call, warm and steady alike, when armed).
#   cpu_x_warm_ns,
#   cpu_x_warm_layers    - the topk_id (and, if fetched this call, x/rw) span(s),
#                          but for a call whose own try_acquire_simultaneous
#                          invocation enqueued a fill_weights_memory upload (a
#                          new bool* uploads_enqueued out-parameter on
#                          try_acquire_simultaneous, expert_weight_
#                          provider.hpp/expert_weight_providers.{hpp,cpp}) --
#                          i.e. a warm-up call, the only kind that ever
#                          uploads once patches/0012's pool-full policy makes
#                          the resident/CPU-tier partition static. us:
#                          avg_cpu_x_warm_us. Kept out of the steady-state
#                          counters above so warm-up's queued-upload cost
#                          (design's H2 hypothesis) does not bias the average
#                          the P* pass bar is judged against.
#   avg_cpu_x_us         - EXISTING counter (cpu_x_readback_ns), divisor
#                          changed from cpu_tier_layers to cpu_tier_calls -
#                          cpu_x_warm_layers ("cpu_steady_layers" in
#                          moe_otd_runtime.cpp): the population it sums over
#                          changed (every steady tier-on call by default, not
#                          just the ones with a miss), so the old divisor
#                          would silently under-count and inflate the
#                          average. Stated here because a silent divisor
#                          change is exactly the kind of thing this
#                          repository's measurement-discipline rule exists to
#                          catch, and re-verified by this patch's dump()-
#                          divisor unit test (see "Unit tests" below).
#
# Env switches (both follow MOE_OTD_FORCE_FINISH/MOE_OTD_PERF_LOG's existing
# convention: a static const bool from std::getenv(...) != nullptr, so the
# switch arms on the variable being PRESENT in the environment at all, any
# value including an empty one -- MOE_OTD_READBACK_PROBE= or =0 still arms
# it):
#   MOE_OTD_READBACK_PROBE=1    - arms the cpu_x_drain_ns probe above. Costs a
#                                  real stream.finish() every tier-on call;
#                                  never set in the steady P* measurement,
#                                  only in the dedicated discriminator run
#                                  below.
#   MOE_OTD_READBACK_NOHOIST=1  - restores patches/0012's read order (see
#                                  "What changed and why" above) so the
#                                  hoist's win and the topk_id leg alone are
#                                  both measurable from one build.
#   MOE_OTD_PERF_LOG=1          - unchanged (patches/0004); the existing
#                                  opt-in that makes get_perf_counters()
#                                  return non-null and prints the one
#                                  [OTD_PERF] line at exit.
#
# Measurement recipe (design note §3), for the orchestrator's GPU window, not
# run here:
#   1. Tier-off vacuity: P* with --moe-cpu-tier absent, MOE_OTD_PERF_LOG=1.
#      Every counter this patch adds must read 0 in the [OTD_PERF] line.
#   2. Steady-state decomposition: P* with --moe-cpu-tier, MOE_OTD_PERF_LOG=1,
#      neither MOE_OTD_READBACK_PROBE nor MOE_OTD_READBACK_NOHOIST set. Read
#      avg_cpu_x_us (compare to the 283 us on record), avg_cpu_topk_id_us,
#      avg_cpu_x_enq_us, avg_cpu_x_wait_us, avg_cpu_x_warm_us,
#      cpu_x_warm_layers, avg_cpu_compute_us, avg_cpu_y_us,
#      avg_cpu_pairs_per_layer, gpu_hit_rate, end-to-end t/s. Assert
#      avg_cpu_x_enq_us + avg_cpu_x_wait_us == avg_cpu_x_us (the per-call
#      arithmetic identity, so it must hold on the averages within
#      rounding); cpu_tier_calls == 40 (layers) x 64 (P*'s decode tokens) =
#      2560 (every tier-on call is counted, not just the miss ones); and
#      cpu_tier_calls > cpu_tier_layers (a run where every call misses would
#      make the two equal, which P*'s ratio-50 pool is not expected to do).
#   3. Nohoist comparison: same cell, same flags as step 2, plus
#      MOE_OTD_READBACK_NOHOIST=1. Read avg_cpu_topk_id_us (now the blocking
#      topk_id round trip alone) and avg_cpu_x_us (now the separate,
#      miss-gated x/rw wait alone). avg_cpu_topk_id_us(nohoist) +
#      avg_cpu_x_us(nohoist) vs avg_cpu_x_us(step 2, hoisted) is the hoist's
#      measured saving.
#   4. Drain discriminator: same cell, same flags as step 2, plus
#      MOE_OTD_READBACK_PROBE=1. Read avg_cpu_x_drain_us. Close to the
#      pre-existing 283 us baseline means the cost was queue backlog (H2),
#      not the readback itself, and 0018's residency change is misaimed --
#      the design's own "decisive discriminator."
#   5. Fresh process per configuration (this repository's depth-sweep rule):
#      runs 1-4 are four separate processes, not four flag toggles in one.
#
# Measured (orchestrator's GPU window, host quiet -- both card-owning units
# stopped for the window). Cell, named in full because it is NOT P* above
# (P*'s prompt is 897 tokens; this run's is 1,198 -- every number below
# belongs to THIS cell, stated once here rather than re-qualified per line):
# 16 GiB card, 35B int4, otd_ratio 50, 8 GiB device pool, 128 slots, u8 KV,
# one lane, n_ctx 65,536, a 1,198-token prompt, 64 greedy decode tokens, two
# runs per process, one process per arm (so cpu_tier_calls' expected 40 x 128
# below is 40 layers x 128 decode tokens, two 64-token runs in one process).
#   1. Tier-off vacuity: holds -- every counter this patch adds reads 0.
#      2.6 t/s.
#   2. Steady-state decomposition: avg_cpu_x_us 3,651 = avg_cpu_x_enq_us 184
#      + avg_cpu_x_wait_us 3,467 (the enq+wait==readback identity holds).
#      cpu_tier_calls 5,120 = 40 x 128 (first recipe assertion holds);
#      cpu_steady_layers 5,120, cpu_x_warm_layers 0; cpu_tier_calls (5,120)
#      > cpu_tier_layers (5,074) (second recipe assertion holds). 4.8/5.0
#      t/s against the pre-0017 baseline's 4.7/4.7 on this cell.
#   3. Drain discriminator: avg_cpu_x_drain_us 3,511; in this same
#      drain-probe run, once the drain itself is excluded, the merged
#      topk_id+x+rw wait measures only avg_cpu_x_enq_us 108 +
#      avg_cpu_x_wait_us 106 = 214 us total -- against the drain's 3,511 us
#      and step 2's undrained 3,651 us, essentially the whole cost is queue
#      backlog (H2), not the readback itself; the 0018 residency change is
#      misaimed on this evidence.
#   4. Nohoist comparison: avg_cpu_topk_id_us 3,620 for the blocking topk_id
#      read alone; the separate, miss-gated x/routing-weight readback that
#      follows is 53 us (avg_cpu_x_enq_us 13 + avg_cpu_x_wait_us 40, still
#      summing correctly). Reading: patches/0012's original 283-291 us was
#      never mostly an x transfer cost -- it was already queue drain
#      (matching H2/step 3), left over from a topk_id read that pays the
#      same ~3,500-3,700 us regardless of whether x/rw ride along with it
#      (step 2) or wait until after try_acquire and the slot upload (this
#      step). usm_host destinations (A1) account for essentially the whole
#      throughput gain over pre-0017: the pair 4.8/5.0 (step 2's two runs)
#      against pre-0017's pair 4.7/4.7 is about +4%; citing the single
#      numbers alone (5.0 vs 4.7) would read +6%, overstating it. The hoist
#      (A2) measures as a null on top of A1 (5.0 t/s hoisted vs 5.0 t/s
#      nohoist, both with usm_host destinations) -- on this cell A2 bought
#      nothing A1 had not already bought.
#   5. Fresh process per arm: as specified.
#   Byte-identity: all six arms (tier off, pre-0017 tier ON x2, this patch's
#   tier ON, nohoist, drain probe) byte-identical over the 64 greedy tokens;
#   exactly forty `cpu_tier on:` lines in every tier-on log. The acceptance
#   task's 10/10 gate was not part of this window and is still open.
#
# Open discrepancy, unexplained by this window: this cell ran at 2.6 t/s
# (tier off) / 5.0 t/s (tier on) against the 10.4 / 15.0 t/s on record for
# P* -- not host load, clocks, or the PCIe link (this window
# ruled those out) -- with the expert-fetch totals (total_disk_io ~= 9.3 s,
# total_gpu_copy ~= 9 s, both per 128 decode tokens) named as the next thing
# to compare, not yet done.
#
# Still open, not superseded by the Measured block above (which already
# covers arm (a) -- tier off vs tier on, same cell -- and the forty-line
# count, both measured there): this patch's tier-ON text against the
# recorded pre-0017 tier-ON text at P* itself (P*, not the measured cell
# above, whose 1,198-token prompt differs from P*'s 897); and the
# acceptance task 10/10 with the tier on at P*. This patch changes no
# arithmetic anywhere in moe_cpu_expert.hpp's kernel or the GEMV kernels'
# sentinel skip -- only where three existing reads sit relative to each
# other and what memory type two of them land in -- so the claim is that
# these two remaining gates hold for the same reason patches/0012's did,
# not new reasoning; the orchestrator's job is to run them, not re-derive
# them.
#
# Unit tests, host-only (tests/unit/test_cases/moe_3gemm_gpu_test.cpp, suite
# moe_otd_perf_counters): both construct an OtdPerfCounters directly -- no
# engine, no stream, no GPU device, so both run in any world this
# environment can reach, this one included, and neither was run on a card
# here (this task's rule; the orchestrator's window runs them alongside
# T2/T4 the way it always has).
#   enq_plus_wait_equals_readback: calls record_cpu_topk_id_timing()/
#     record_cpu_x_timing() (moe_otd_perf.hpp) with three synthetic
#     std::chrono::steady_clock time points. A construction lock, not a
#     red-first test: given this patch's actual on_before_batched_gemv there
#     is no reachable call site that could violate the asserted invariants
#     (the hoisted path always feeds both functions the same span; enq+wait
#     is two halves of one interval by record_cpu_x_timing's own body) --
#     run today, every assertion is green, which is proof of the
#     construction holding, not evidence a defect was found. What it locks
#     against, reasoned rather than found: a future edit computing
#     cpu_x_wait_ns from the wrong pair of timestamps, e.g. ns(tx0,
#     t_wait_done) instead of ns(t_enq_done, t_wait_done). Against the
#     test's synthetic 50/233/283 us split that specific bug sums to
#     333000 ns instead of 283000 ns, tripping the first EXPECT_EQ.
#   dump_avg_cpu_x_us_uses_steady_layer_divisor: sets cpu_tier_calls=40,
#     cpu_x_warm_layers=8, cpu_tier_layers=12, cpu_x_readback_ns=32*283000 ns
#     on a fresh OtdPerfCounters, captures dump()'s stderr line (std::cerr
#     rdbuf swap), and asserts it contains "avg_cpu_x_us=283". Red against
#     patches/0012's own divisor by construction, not hypothetically: with
#     0012's cpu_tier_layers-based divisor the same inputs print
#     avg_cpu_x_us=754 (9056/12, integer division) -- this test would have
#     failed against that code, and the second EXPECT_EQ checks the string
#     "avg_cpu_x_us=754" is absent from the line this patch's dump()
#     produces.
#
# Deviations from the design note, stated rather than hidden:
#   - §2 A2's text names only "the x copy" next to topk_id ("enqueue the x
#     copy non-blocking with the topk_id copy and join both in one
#     wait_for_events"). This patch folds the routing-weight copy into the
#     same wait too. "x" is used as shorthand for the x+routing-weight
#     bundle throughout §1 (cpu_x_readback_ns/avg_cpu_x_us already reported
#     both DMAs as one number before this patch, since patches/0012 joined
#     them); leaving routing weights stranded at the old step 4 would have
#     reintroduced exactly the second round trip A2 exists to remove, for a
#     payload of a few dozen bytes. §4's "Touch" list confirms A2 is in
#     0017's scope; this patch reads the bundling as inherited from the
#     status quo it was written against, not as a narrower instruction.
#   - clone()'s explicit field list (moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt_impl::clone(),
#     flagged by §4 as "0012's dormant-tier defect lives exactly there") is
#     reviewed, not extended: the two new member pairs
#     (_cpu_x_host_mem/_cpu_x_host_capacity, _cpu_rw_host_mem/
#     _cpu_rw_host_capacity) are lazily-allocated scratch, default-null and
#     zero-capacity on a fresh clone exactly like the std::vector members
#     they replace, and exactly like _cpu_out_rows/_cpu_jobs/_cpu_descs
#     (already scratch, already absent from that list). Unlike _cpu_tier and
#     its sibling configuration flags -- the actual 0012 defect, where an
#     unlisted field left the tier configured on the program's impl and
#     dormant on the one that executed -- nothing new here is configuration.
#   - expert_weight_provider.hpp's uploads_enqueued out-parameter: the design
#     specifies the out-flag but not its behaviour on an early return. This
#     patch's choice (both implementations, review round): write false on
#     entry, before any possible early exit including a capacity-exceeded
#     nullopt return, then overwrite with the real answer on every path that
#     resolves a lease -- never left untouched on some return path and set
#     only on others, so a caller's local bool need not be pre-initialized to
#     get a well-defined value regardless of which path returns.
#
# Packaging (CHANGELOG.md, contrib/packaging/marfrit-openvino) deferred to
# the next release level, same as patches/0015-0016.
#
# Do NOT touch, unchanged by this patch: the kernel sentinel skip in
# moe_3gemm_swiglu_mlp.cl; the admission policy in try_acquire_simultaneous
# (0012 §2.4's rejected A/B/C stay rejected); the CPU kernels themselves
# (moe_cpu_expert.hpp, patches/0011); mlp_reduce; the prefill path
# (on_before_prefill/build_grouped_mask_otd keep passing nullptr for both
# try_acquire_simultaneous out-parameters, unaffected by either new
# parameter's default); the LRU and eviction policy; paged-KV (patches
# 0008-0010); the residency/host-visible-hidden_states change (0018).
diff --git a/src/plugins/intel_gpu/src/graph/impls/ocl_v2/moe/expert_weight_provider.hpp b/src/plugins/intel_gpu/src/graph/impls/ocl_v2/moe/expert_weight_provider.hpp
index ecbc13c..4f3cac9 100644
--- a/src/plugins/intel_gpu/src/graph/impls/ocl_v2/moe/expert_weight_provider.hpp
+++ b/src/plugins/intel_gpu/src/graph/impls/ocl_v2/moe/expert_weight_provider.hpp
@@ -84,10 +84,23 @@ public:
